Marwaris in #Sikkim: 
A Journey of 140 Years of Trust, Enterprise and Service (1888-2026)

By S.K.SARDA
(Founder president of sikkim marwari samaj)

"We Came as Traders, We Became Partners in Sikkim's Progress."

The celebration of 140 years of the Marwari community in Sikkim is not merely the commemoration of the arrival of a trading community in a Himalayan kingdom. It is the celebration of a remarkable journey of mutual trust, shared prosperity and harmonious coexistence between the people of Sikkim and the Marwari community over nearly one hundred and forty  years.

Today, as we gather to celebrate our journey , we pay tribute not only to our forefathers but also to the enlightened rulers, visionary administrators and the warm-hearted people of Sikkim who welcomed our ancestors and enabled them to become an integral part of this beautiful land.

The Beginning

The association of the Marwari community with Sikkim dates back to around 1885–1888, when few enterprising traders established the first permanent shops on Ridge Road near the Royal Palace at Gangtok.

Those early pioneers travelled across difficult mountain terrain with limited resources but unlimited determination. There were no motor roads, no trucks, no telephones and no modern banking. Goods were transported by mule caravans ,bull cart and porters over mountains linking Sikkim with Darjeeling, Kalimpong and  Jalpaiguri.. Every trading expedition demanded courage, patience and perseverance.
Their enterprise laid the foundations of organised commerce in the the then  Kingdom.

A Relationship Built on Trust

One of the most remarkable features of Sikkim's history has been the atmosphere of trust that existed between the Chogyals and the trading community.
The successive Chogyals appreciated honest enterprise and encouraged commerce as an essential pillar of the Kingdom's development. In return, the Marwari community contributed not merely as traders but as responsible citizens who respected the traditions, customs and values of Sikkim.

That relationship of confidence became one of the cornerstones of the Kingdom's economic growth.
Building Markets and Connecting Communities
The planned development of Gangtok Bazaar during the early twentieth century marked an important stage in Sikkim's commercial history.

Over the decades, markets flourished at Gangtok, Deorali, Tadong, Ranipool, Singtam, Rangpo, Mangan, Dikchu, Namchi, Jorethang, Geyzing and many other towns. Weekly haats connected farmers directly with consumers and encouraged the marketing of local produce.

The establishment of Lal Bazaar in 1955 further strengthened agricultural marketing and provided cultivators with a dependable marketplace.

These commercial centres gradually became centres of social interaction, cultural exchange and economic progress.

Trade Across the Himalayas

For centuries, Sikkim occupied a unique geographical position between the Tibetan Plateau and the Indian plains.

Trade through Jelep La and Nathu La connected Sikkim with Tibet and beyond. During the 1950s, Marwari traders from Gangtok and Kalimpong established business houses in Yatung and Phari, participating actively in the flourishing Indo-Tibetan trade.

Sikkim's renowned oranges, large cardamom, medicinal herbs and forest products reached markets across India and beyond, while essential goods travelled into the Kingdom from Indian plains.

Partners in Modern Development

The reign of His Excellency Chogyal Palden Thondup Namgyal marked an important chapter in the modernization of Sikkim.

Education expanded rapidly.
Healthcare facilities improved.
Roads connected remote villages.
Power generation increased.

Industries such as Temi Tea, cottage industries, fruit preservation and mining were strengthened.

The Marwari community actively participated in this transformation by expanding commerce, transport, hospitality and service enterprises while generating employment and supporting economic growth.

The relationship between visionary governance and responsible entrepreneurship became one of the strengths of Sikkim's development.

Beyond Business

The contribution of the Marwari community has never been confined to commerce alone.

Over the decades, community members have supported educational institutions, healthcare facilities, charitable organizations, sports, cultural activities and religious institutions.

The Thakurbari, Hanuman Temple, Satsang Hall, Shanti Bhawan, Blood donation camps and numerous philanthropic initiatives reflect the community's commitment to public welfare.

Quiet acts of charity, scholarships for deserving students, medical assistance and relief during natural disasters have formed an integral part of this tradition of service.

Harmony: Sikkim's Greatest Strength

Perhaps the greatest lesson Sikkim offers the nation is the example of peaceful coexistence.

Here, people belonging to different communities, faiths and languages have lived together with mutual respect and affection.

The Marwari community has always considered itself an integral part of this harmonious social fabric.
While preserving our heritage, language and traditions, we have also embraced the culture, festivals and aspirations of Sikkim with equal pride.

This spirit of inclusiveness remains one of the greatest strengths of our State.

Looking Towards the Future

As we celebrate one hundred and forty years of our journey, we also look ahead.

The next generation has new responsibilities.
They must build enterprises based on innovation, create employment, support education, promote sustainable tourism, protect the fragile Himalayan environment and contribute to the knowledge economy.

Our forefathers crossed mountains with determination.
The youth of today must cross the frontiers of technology, research and entrepreneurship with the same courage and integrity.

A Shared Heritage

The story of the Marwaris in Sikkim is not the story of one community alone. It is a chapter in the larger history of Sikkim itself.

It is a story of trust .
Of cooperation over competition.
Of service over self-interest.
Of partnership over separation.

For nearly one hundred and forty years, the #Marwari community has shared the joys, challenges and aspirations of the people of Sikkim.

As we celebrate this historic milestone, we bow with gratitude to our pioneering ancestors, to the enlightened Chogyals whose encouragement fostered commerce and, to the Post 1975 Governments that have continued to nurture an environment of progress, and above all to the people of Sikkim, whose friendship and affection have been our greatest strength.

History teaches us that prosperous societies are built not merely by governments but by partnerships between enlightened leadership and enterprising citizens. The history of Sikkim bears testimony to this truth. The vision of the Chogyals, the democratic leadership that followed, and the participation of all communities—including the Marwaris—together transformed  Sikkim into one of India's most progressive mountain States. This shared legacy belongs equally to every Sikkimese.

May this bond continue to grow stronger in the years ahead.

Jai Sikkim.  Jai Hind

